Code Generation and Implementation Details:
For each unimplemented API endpoint, create or update the corresponding PHP controller class in the plugins/bjt-core-entities/controllers/ directory.
Follow the existing coding patterns:
New controllers should extend the BJT_API_Controller base class (presumably located in plugins/bjt-core-entities/includes/class-api-controller.php).
Routes for new endpoints must be registered within the register_routes() method of their respective controllers.
Ensure new controller classes are added to the $controllers array in plugins/bjt-core-entities/bjt-product-api.php to be initialized.
Implement the business logic for each endpoint. This will involve:
Reading request parameters, headers, and body.
Validating input data.
Performing database operations (SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) using WordPress database functions (global $wpdb;) interacting with tables defined in mockup.sql. Ensure to use prepared statements to prevent SQL injection.
Formatting the response according to the JSON structure specified in frontend/src/API 接口文档.ini (including success, data, message, code fields).
Implementing authentication checks where required (refer to "Authorization: Bearer {token}" in API docs).
Implementing pagination, multi-language (lang parameter), and multi-region (region parameter) support as defined in the API documentation.
Properly handling errors and returning appropriate error responses and codes.
Update Documentation:
After implementing and verifying each set of endpoints, update the plugins/bjt-core-entities/.cursor/IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md file to accurately reflect the completion status (mark implemented endpoints with ✅ and update completion percentages).
Testing and Verification:
The goal is for all implemented API endpoints to be fully functional and to pass all existing project-specific test scripts.
While the test scripts themselves are not provided in this context, the generated code should be written with testability in mind, adhering strictly to the API specifications to ensure compatibility with any tests based on that specification.
Manually verify basic functionality of each endpoint using a tool like Postman or curl, considering different scenarios (valid requests, invalid requests, edge cases).
